    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One thing I'm worried about is the potential for \"orphaned\" items or items that don't quite fit into the established subclasses. If we have a subset of manuscripts that are particularly fragmented or don't follow a clear thematic line, how does the LCC handle that kind of structural chaos compared to Dewey? I want to avoid a situation where the collection just becomes a disorganized mess over time.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under LCC, each item is assigned a call number, which functions as a unique identifier. The system can alleviate the issue of \"orphaned\" items by allowing you to create subject headings that still fall under less conventional or multifaceted topics. LCC’s hierarchy is generally designed such that even rarities or outliers typically have a place, as opposed to the DDC, which might categorize unique items differently and obscuring their multifaceted nature. As a result, LCC maintains clarity even in a disorganized collection, and the detail-oriented metadata offers multiple pathways for discovery, thereby minimizing the chance of items becoming invisible within the archive.\n\n\nMoreover, with a larger number of subclasses, the LCC is better equipped to categorize documents according to multiple criteria. You can cross-classify manuscripts with several appropriate classifications, ensuring that although they might not fit neatly into a singular class, they are still indexed and accessible. This flexibility is one of the strengths of LCC, particularly for a university archive that is likely to have a diverse range of materials that might not fit neatly into traditional categories. Properly utilizing the LCC’s descriptive framework, your collection can be both organized and accessible despite its potentially chaotic nature.",
      "source": "target",
